  "summary": "Hospital operating margins deteriorated significantly in July, dropping to 1.4% from 2.2% in the prior two months as elective surgery volumes declined seasonally and payer mix pressures mounted, signaling ongoing financial strain in the health system. On the technology front, the FDA cleared Beta Bionics' Mint patch pump while Medicare is expanding its pilot program that reimburses technology for chronic disease management, reflecting broader momentum toward digital health adoption. Meanwhile, governance challenges around agentic AI access and the rushed rollout of Medicare's AI prior-authorization system—which caused delayed care in some cases—highlight the tension between innovation velocity and implementation readiness in healthcare IT. Venture funding remained robust with Thatch reaching unicorn status at $1 billion valuation on a $108 million Series C, and GenHealth.ai raising $16.5 million for healthcare AI agents. On the biotech side, GSK acquired global rights to a tri-specific T cell engager from Chimagen for multiple myeloma while Vera's kidney drug Trutakna showed strong Phase 3 results, and obesity drugmakers are now testing treatments in children as young as six. Finally, Pennsylvania reported a fourth measles death, underscoring continued public health concerns in undervaccinated populations.",
